.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt UNIX 4
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm unix
.Nd local interprocess communication sockets
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/socket.h
.In sys/un.h
.Ft int
.Fn socket AF_UNIX SOCK_STREAM 0
.Ft int
.Fn socket AF_UNIX SOCK_DGRAM 0
.Ft int
.Fn socket AF_UNIX SOCK_SEQPACKET 0
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Local sockets connect processes on the same machine.
They are created with
.Xr socket 2
or
.Xr socketpair 2
in the domain
.Dv AF_UNIX
.Pq or Dv AF_LOCAL ,
with one of three types:
.Bl -tag -width SOCK_SEQPACKET
.It Dv SOCK_STREAM
a connected byte stream.
.It Dv SOCK_DGRAM
unconnected messages, which keep their boundaries.
.It Dv SOCK_SEQPACKET
a connected stream of records, which keep their boundaries; every record
received carries
.Dv MSG_EOR .
.El
.Pp
The protocol must be 0.
.Ss Addresses
An address is a
.Vt struct sockaddr_un :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct sockaddr_un {
	sa_family_t	sun_family;	/* AF_UNIX */
	char		sun_path[108];
};
.Ed
.Pp
The address length given with it decides which of three kinds of name it
holds:
.Bl -tag -width "Path name"
.It Unnamed
The length is
.Li sizeof(sa_family_t) .
.It Path name
The first byte of
.Fa sun_path
is not NUL.
The name is the bytes up to the first NUL or the end of the length given,
at most 107 of them.
.It Abstract
The first byte of
.Fa sun_path
is NUL and the length is longer than
.Li sizeof(sa_family_t) .
The name is exactly the bytes after that first NUL up to the length given;
further NUL bytes are part of it.
.El
.Pp
.Xr bind 2
to a path name creates a file of type
.Dv S_IFSOCK
at the path, with mode
.Li 0777
less the process's umask, owned by the caller.
It fails with
.Er EADDRINUSE
if anything exists at the path, a dangling symbolic link included.
The file names the socket, not the reverse: closing the socket leaves the
file, which then refuses connections
.Pq Er ECONNREFUSED ,
and removing the file leaves connected peers working.
A socket file left from an earlier boot refuses connections too; remove it
with
.Xr unlink 2
before binding again.
.Xr connect 2
and sends to a path name need write permission on the file.
.Xr open 2
on a socket file fails with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
.Pp
Abstract names, from Linux, are not files.
They exist while a socket is bound to them, carry no permissions, are the
same inside a
.Xr chroot 2 ,
and are released when the socket is closed.
.Pp
Binding with an unnamed address binds the socket to a fresh abstract name
of five lowercase hexadecimal digits, as Linux does.
A socket that sends a datagram without being bound sends it unnamed.
.Pp
.Xr getsockname 2 ,
.Xr getpeername 2 ,
.Xr accept 2
and
.Xr recvmsg 2
return an address length of
.Li offsetof(struct sockaddr_un, sun_path)
plus the path's length plus one for a path name,
.Li sizeof(sa_family_t)
for an unnamed socket, and
.Li sizeof(sa_family_t)
plus one plus the name's length for an abstract name.
A path name is returned as it was given to
.Xr bind 2 ,
relative or not.
The side of a connection returned by
.Xr accept 2
has the listener's name.
.Ss Connections
.Xr listen 2
on a bound stream or sequenced-packet socket queues at most
.Fa backlog
connections not yet accepted, and never more than 128; a backlog of zero
or less means 1.
.Xr connect 2
to a listening socket completes at once: the connection enters the
listener's queue, and both sides may send immediately.
When the queue is full it fails with
.Er ECONNREFUSED
instead of waiting, as in the BSDs.
.Pp
Each direction of a connection has one buffer, whose size is the
receiver's
.Dv SO_RCVBUF
(64 KiB by default, 512 bytes to 1 MiB).
A send blocks while there is no room in it.
A sequenced-packet record larger than the buffer fails with
.Er EMSGSIZE .
.Pp
On a stream, a receive never returns bytes from two sends when the later one
carried control data: control data stays with the bytes it was sent with.
.Pp
After
.Li shutdown(SHUT_WR)
the peer reads end-of-file once it has read what was sent; after
.Li shutdown(SHUT_RD)
the data queued and the data arriving are discarded, and reads return
end-of-file.
When one side closes, the other reads what remains and then end-of-file,
and its sends fail with
.Er EPIPE
and raise
.Dv SIGPIPE
.Po
unless
.Dv MSG_NOSIGNAL
or
.Dv SO_NOSIGPIPE
is set
.Pc .
Connections still queued when a listener is closed are reset: reads on
their other ends fail with
.Er ECONNRESET .
.Ss Datagrams
A datagram larger than the sender's
.Dv SO_SNDBUF
(64 KiB by default) fails with
.Er EMSGSIZE .
A receiving socket queues at most its
.Dv SO_RCVBUF
bytes; a datagram that does not fit is dropped and its send fails with
.Er ENOBUFS ,
as in the BSDs.
A datagram longer than the receive buffer given is truncated and
.Dv MSG_TRUNC
set.
.Pp
.Xr connect 2
on a datagram socket sets the destination of sends without an address; it
does not filter what the socket receives.
Connecting to an address of family
.Dv AF_UNSPEC
removes it.
Once the destination's socket is closed, sends without an address fail with
.Er ENOTCONN .
.Ss Descriptor passing
A control message of level
.Dv SOL_SOCKET
and type
.Dv SCM_RIGHTS
holding an array of
.Vt int
sends the open file descriptions those descriptors refer to.
The kernel holds them from the send until they are received or the
message is discarded, so the sender may close its descriptors at once.
The receiver gets new descriptors, the lowest ones free, with the
close-on-exec flag set if it passed
.Dv MSG_CMSG_CLOEXEC .
.Pp
A receive whose control buffer is too small for all the descriptors
installs as many as fit, closes the rest, and sets
.Dv MSG_CTRUNC .
The padding
.Fn CMSG_SPACE
adds after the last control message counts as room: a buffer of
.Li CMSG_SPACE(sizeof(int))
bytes takes two descriptors, as on Linux.
.Xr read 2 ,
which has no control buffer, closes every descriptor sent with the data it
returns.
A receive with
.Dv MSG_PEEK
leaves the descriptors in the message for the next receive.
A message discarded unread
.Po
by
.Li shutdown(SHUT_RD) ,
by closing its socket, or by a datagram that could not be queued
.Pc
closes the descriptions it carried.
.Pp
A socket sent over itself, or a cycle of sockets sent over each other, can
become reachable only through the messages in its own queue.
When a descriptor of a socket with descriptions in flight is closed, the
kernel collects such sockets, as the BSDs'
.Fn unp_gc
does, and closes them.
.Pp
At most 1024 descriptions sent by one user, and 4096 in all, may be in
flight at once; a send beyond either limit fails with
.Er ETOOMANYREFS .
The superuser is held to the system-wide limit only.
.Ss Credentials
A process's credentials here are its process ID, user ID and group ID.
Until effective and saved IDs exist, the effective IDs equal the real ones,
and the group list is the single group ID.
.Pp
Each end of a connection records its peer's credentials when the
connection is made: the connecting side the listener's as of
.Xr listen 2 ,
the accepted side the connector's as of
.Xr connect 2 ,
and each end of a
.Xr socketpair 2
its creator's.
They are read with
.Xr getpeereid 3 ,
or with these options:
.Bl -tag -width LOCAL_PEERCRED
.It Dv LOCAL_PEERCRED
Level
.Dv SOL_LOCAL ,
read only.
A
.Vt struct xucred
from
.In sys/ucred.h ,
with
.Va cr_version
set to
.Dv XUCRED_VERSION ,
.Va cr_uid ,
.Va cr_ngroups
(1),
.Va cr_groups
and
.Va cr_pid .
.It Dv SO_PEERCRED
Level
.Dv SOL_SOCKET ,
read only, from Linux.
A
.Vt struct ucred :
.Va pid ,
.Va uid ,
.Va gid .
.El
.Pp
Both fail with
.Er ENOTCONN
on a socket that was never connected.
.Pp
Credentials can also travel with each message, in three forms:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Dv SCM_CREDS No from the sender
A sender that includes a control message of type
.Dv SCM_CREDS
has its contents replaced by the kernel with its own
.Vt struct cmsgcred
.Pq Va cmcred_pid , cmcred_uid , cmcred_euid , cmcred_gid , cmcred_ngroups , cmcred_groups ,
which the receiver gets as sent.
Whatever the sender wrote there is ignored, so it cannot be forged.
.It Dv SO_PASSCRED
From Linux.
A receiver with this
.Dv SOL_SOCKET
option set gets an
.Dv SCM_CREDENTIALS
control message holding a
.Vt struct ucred
with every message.
A sender may supply one itself, which is refused with
.Er EPERM
unless it holds the sender's own process, user and group IDs or the sender
is the superuser; without one, the kernel supplies the sender's.
.It Dv LOCAL_CREDS
Level
.Dv SOL_LOCAL .
The receiver gets an
.Dv SCM_CREDS
control message holding the sender's
.Vt struct sockcred
.Pq Va sc_uid , sc_euid , sc_gid , sc_egid , sc_ngroups , sc_groups :
with every message on a datagram socket, and with the first receive only on
a stream or sequenced-packet socket.
.It Dv LOCAL_CREDS_PERSISTENT
Level
.Dv SOL_LOCAL .
The receiver gets an
.Dv SCM_CREDS2
control message holding a
.Vt struct sockcred2 ,
which adds
.Va sc_version
and
.Va sc_pid ,
with every message.
.El
.Pp
.Dv LOCAL_CREDS
and
.Dv LOCAL_CREDS_PERSISTENT
exclude each other: setting one while the other is set fails with
.Er EINVAL .
While either is set, an
.Dv SCM_CREDS
message from the sender is dropped, so the receiver sees one set of
credentials, the kernel's.
A receive with
.Dv MSG_PEEK
shows the credentials without using up the one that
.Dv LOCAL_CREDS
gives a stream.
.Pp
The credential options belong to local sockets; on a socket of another
family they fail with
.Er EINVAL .
The values of
.Dv SOL_LOCAL
.Pq 0x200 ,
.Dv LOCAL_PEERCRED
.Pq 0x1001 ,
.Dv LOCAL_CREDS
.Pq 0x1002
and
.Dv LOCAL_CREDS_PERSISTENT
.Pq 0x1003
are OxideBSD's own:
.Fx Ns 's
0 and 1 to 3 are
.Dv SOL_IP
and
.Dv SO_*
values in this C library.
.Dv SCM_CREDS
.Pq 3
and
.Dv SCM_CREDS2
.Pq 8
keep
.Fx Ns 's
values.
.Sh ERRORS
Besides those of the socket calls themselves, local sockets report:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EACCES
Search permission is denied on a directory of the path, write permission on
the directory where
.Xr bind 2
would create the file, or write permission on the socket file for
.Xr connect 2
or a send.
.It Bq Er EADDRINUSE
Something exists at the path, or the abstract name is bound.
.It Bq Er EAFNOSUPPORT
The address's family is not
.Dv AF_UNIX .
.It Bq Er ECONNREFUSED
No socket is bound to the name, the socket is not listening, or its queue
is full.
.It Bq Er ECONNRESET
The listener closed before accepting the connection.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The address is shorter than a family or longer than a
.Vt struct sockaddr_un ;
.Xr bind 2
was called on a socket that already has a name;
.Xr listen 2
was called on an unbound or connected socket; or a control message is
malformed or of an unsupported type.
.It Bq Er EISCONN
A connected stream or sequenced-packet socket was given a destination
address, or
.Xr connect 2
was called on it again.
.It Bq Er EMSGSIZE
A datagram is larger than
.Dv SO_SNDBUF ,
or a record larger than the receiver's buffer.
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
The path is 108 bytes or longer.
.It Bq Er ENOBUFS
The receiving datagram socket's queue has no room for the message.
.It Bq Er ENOTCONN
The socket is not connected, or a datagram socket's destination has closed.
.It Bq Er ENOTSOCK
The path names a file that is not a socket.
.It Bq Er EOPNOTSUPP
An unconnected stream or sequenced-packet socket was given a destination
address,
.Xr listen 2
was called on a datagram socket, or
.Dv MSG_OOB
was given.
.It Bq Er EPIPE
The socket's write side is shut down, or its peer has closed.
.It Bq Er EPROTOTYPE
The socket named is of another type.
.It Bq Er ETOOMANYREFS
Too many descriptions are in flight.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr accept 2 ,
.Xr bind 2 ,
.Xr connect 2 ,
.Xr getsockopt 2 ,
.Xr listen 2 ,
.Xr recvmsg 2 ,
.Xr sendmsg 2 ,
.Xr shutdown 2 ,
.Xr socket 2 ,
.Xr socketpair 2 ,
.Xr getpeereid 3
.Sh HISTORY
Local sockets first appeared in
.Bx 4.2 .
OxideBSD's use the C library's Linux-shaped
.Vt struct sockaddr_un ,
without the BSDs'
.Va sun_len ,
and provide Linux's abstract namespace and credential interfaces alongside
the BSD ones.
